{"results": [{"id": "hp_doc_d241c305957bed01db508d78ee7ae30ca58c0febe66de3afa6e498d180533426", "title": "Eran-Khwarrah-Yazdegerd", "text": "Eran-Khwarrah-Yazdegerd (meaning \"Ērān, glory of Yazdegerd\") was a short-lived province of the Sasanian Empire located in the northern part of the Gorgan province. The province was founded by shah Yazdegerd II (r. 438-457).", "score": 18.71}, {"id": "hp_doc_e1c7a11c478c0286405cb5675a1806a9a03ccb29b62effa9730e5b68194df848", "title": "Mihr Narseh", "text": "Mihr Narseh (Middle Persian: \"Mihr Narsē\"), was a powerful Iranian nobleman from the House of Suren, who served as minister (\"wuzurg framadār\") of the Sasanian Empire during the reigns of the Sasanian kings Yazdegerd I (r. 399-420), Bahram V (420–438), Yazdegerd II (r. 438–457) and Peroz I (r. 457–484).", "score": 15.4424}, {"id": "hp_doc_144cdfa400ba18687195bf74775d6920661ddaf6f6c733a533b301c086ae34ee", "title": "Yazdegerd II", "text": "Yazdegerd II (Middle Persian: 𐭩𐭦𐭣𐭪𐭥𐭲𐭩 \"Yazdākird\", meaning \"made by God\"; <a href=\"Persian%20language\">Persianزدگرد‎ ‎ ), was the sixteenth <a href=\"Sasanian%20Empire\">Sasanian</a> emperor of <a href=\"Iran\">Iran</a>. He was the successor and son of Bahram V (420–438) and reigned from 438 to 457. His name may also be spelled Yazdgard II. His reign was marked by wars against the Byzantine Empire in the west and the Hephthalite Empire in the east, as well as by his efforts and attempts to impose Zoroastrianism on the largest religious minority within the empire, namely the Christians.", "score": 15.0706}], "remaining_searches": 2}
